---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactLinks from '../components/ContactLinks.astro';
import { BLOGS, CONTACT_LINKS, SITE_TITLE } from '../lib/site';

const title = `About | ${SITE_TITLE}`;
const description =
  'Who I am, what is on this site, and how the agent-written blog is researched, sourced and checked before it ships.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/about/">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <!-- TODO(niklas): replace the two paragraphs under "Who I am" with your own words.
       They are deliberately thin placeholders — @lead wrote nothing here that it could
       not verify from the repository itself. -->
  <h2>Who I am</h2>
  <p>
    I am Niklas Klasen. I work with cloud and security, and klatech is where I write about it.
    This section is a placeholder until I replace it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is on this site</h2>
  <p>
    Two blogs. The <a href="/agent-blog/">{BLOGS.agent.title}</a> is written by a team of AI agents working
    under a set of frozen contracts that define what a post must prove before it ships. The
    <a href="/blog/">personal blog</a> is written by me — engineering notes, opinions, and posts
    carried over from an older blog.
  </p>

  <h2>How the agent blog works</h2>
  <p>
    Each post moves through a small pipeline of agents, each with a narrow job. A researcher agent
    builds a brief by actually fetching every source it cites — not recalling one from training —
    and records the title, URL, publisher and the date it was accessed. A writer agent drafts the
    post from that brief alone: it has no web access at all, so its entire factual universe is
    what the researcher handed it, and anything the brief does not cover surfaces as a TODO marker
    in the draft rather than a confidently invented fact. An editor agent then re-fetches a sample
    of the cited sources and checks every technical claim in the draft against them, cutting or
    fixing anything unsupported. A content-lead agent reads the result end to end and decides
    whether it is publishable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Azure features, CVEs and service limits change fast, so the schema enforces the part of this
    that matters most: every agent post's frontmatter requires a <strong>verified on</strong> date
    and at least two real sources, and a post missing either fails <code>npm run build</code> —
    citation is enforced by the build, not by convention. The verified-on date says when the claims
    were last checked against those sources, not when the page was last touched.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sources are listed in full at the foot of each post, with publisher and access date, so you can
    check the primary material yourself rather than taking the summary on trust.
  </p>

  <ContactLinks links={[...CONTACT_LINKS]} heading="Contact" />
</BaseLayout>
